@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title G4 · Ownership sequences: who closes the watch?
hide footbox
$caller(caller, "Program\n(caller)")
$callee(callee, "Library function\n(callee)")
== watch_view borrows the watch ==
note over caller : Before entry: program is responsible for closing the watch.
caller --> callee : At entry: lend the watch for this call
note over caller, callee
  The library uses the watch during the call.
  The program keeps responsibility for closing it.
end note
== watch_close consumes the watch ==
note over caller : Before entry: program is responsible for closing the watch.
caller -> callee : At entry: pass responsibility to the library
note over callee : The library destroys the watch to discharge the obligation.
note over caller, callee
  General consumption rule: transfer happens at entry
  on every outcome, including failure. The callee discharges
  the obligation unless it explicitly returns it as an owned output.
end note
== watch_open returns a new owned watch ==
note over callee : Before delivery: library holds the new watch's obligation.
callee -> caller : At delivery: give the watch and responsibility for closing it
note over caller : After delivery: program is responsible for closing the watch.
note over caller, callee
  If the result is null, the program has no watch to close.
end note
legend bottom
  Three separate possible contracts for the invented watch API.
  Read time downward within each case. Non-normative: C2, C3, C5.
  Actor = calling program; rectangle = library function.
  Solid arrow = transfer responsibility; dashed arrow = lend access.
  These notes track release responsibility; exclusive access has separate rules.
  Strong retention is a separate event: it creates another release obligation
  while the caller keeps its own. No retention event is shown here.
endlegend
@enduml
